# Carne Asada (Food Lab)

Source: J. Kenji López-Alt, "The Best Carne Asada." https://www.seriouseats.com/carne-asada-food-lab-recipe-kenji

## Recipe Name:
Carne Asada (Food Lab dried chile marinade)

## Servings:
6

## Prep Time:
10 minutes

## Cook Time:
20 minutes, plus 3 to 12 hours marinating

## Ingredients:
### Marinade
- 3 whole dried ancho chiles, stems and seeds removed
- 3 whole dried guajillo chiles, stems and seeds removed
- 2 whole chipotle peppers, canned in adobo
- 3/4 cup (180 ml) fresh orange juice, from 2 to 3 oranges. Sour oranges are best.
- 2 tbsp (30 ml) fresh lime juice, from 2 to 3 limes
- 2 tbsp (30 ml) extra-virgin olive oil
- 2 tbsp (30 ml) soy sauce
- 2 tbsp (30 ml) Asian fish sauce, such as Red Boat
- 2 tbsp (30 g) dark brown sugar
- 1 small bunch cilantro, leaves and tender stems only, divided
- 6 medium cloves garlic
- 1 tbsp whole cumin seed, toasted and ground
- 1 tsp whole coriander seed, toasted and ground
- Kosher salt

### Beef
- 2 lb (900 g) skirt steak, 2 to 3 whole steaks, trimmed and cut with the grain into 5- to 6-inch lengths
- 2 tsp extra kosher salt for the marinade, on top of the salt you season the sauce with

### To serve
- Warm corn or flour tortillas, lime wedges, diced onion, fresh cilantro, avocado

Swaps: negro, pasilla, or California chiles can replace the ancho and guajillo at the same weight. Hanger or flap steak can replace the skirt at the same weight. Leave a lot of fat on the skirt; trim only the tough silver skin. Cutting it crosswise with the grain into 5- to 6-inch lengths is what lets you slice against the grain later.

## Instructions:
1. Microwave the dried anchos and guajillos on a microwave-safe plate for 10 to 20 seconds, until pliable and toasty-smelling.
2. Put them in a blender with the chipotles, orange juice, lime juice, olive oil, soy sauce, fish sauce, brown sugar, cilantro, garlic, cumin, and coriander. Blend about 1 minute to a smooth sauce. Season to taste with salt.
3. Pour half into a large bowl and half into a sealed container. Refrigerate the sealed half; that is the salsa you serve with the meat.
4. Add 2 extra teaspoons of salt to the bowl. It should taste saltier than is comfortable. That is about 3 percent salt, and it is what lets the marinade penetrate the meat.
5. Coat each steak in the bowl, then put them all in a gallon zipper bag with the top folded over so marinade stays out of the seal. Pour any extra marinade over the top. Press the air out and seal.
6. Refrigerate 3 to 12 hours. Most of the flavor lands by hour 3. Past 12 hours the acid starts to break the meat down.
7. Light one full chimney of charcoal. Spread the coals on one side of the grate. Set the grate in place, cover, and preheat 5 minutes. Gas grill: half the burners on the highest setting, covered, 10 minutes. Clean and oil the grate.
8. Take the serving salsa out of the fridge so the chill comes off.
9. Wipe the excess marinade off the steaks. Grill right over the hot side. Cover a gas grill; leave a charcoal grill open. Turn occasionally until well charred outside and the center reads 110 F (43 C), 5 to 10 minutes total.
10. Rest 5 minutes on a cutting board. Carryover cooking brings it to 125 to 130 F (52 to 54 C), which is medium-rare.
11. Slice thin against the grain, perpendicular to the striations. Serve with the reserved salsa, lime wedges, avocado, onion, cilantro, and tortillas.

